window.docContents[217].resolve({"/Foundations/#PDEAtlas--Foundations--Coordinates-and-Boundary-Calculus--Euclidean___-polar___-and-spherical-coordinates--Radial-and-planar-differential-coordinates":{"contents":"In dimension , the radial expression is\n. It specializes to the familiar polar and spherical formulas in\ndimensions two and three. Real division is total in Lean, so the definition itself also has a\nvalue at zero; analytic theorems interpreting it as a coordinate Laplacian assume that the\nradius is positive or nonzero.By the radial Laplacian theorem,\nthis expression is the Cartesian Laplacian of  at\n, provided the profile is twice continuously differentiable at that radius.\n\nThe two profiles that matter for fundamental solutions are the logarithm in\ndimension two and powers of the radius in higher dimensions. For the logarithm\nthe two terms of the two-dimensional radial Laplacian cancel exactly.\n\nOn the positive radial branch,This one-variable identity underlies the two-dimensional logarithmic\nfundamental solution away from its singularity.\n\nDifferentiate on the positive half-line:  and\n. Substitution into the two-dimensional radial\nLaplacian gives .\n\nFor a power profile the radial Laplacian is again a power, with a coefficient\nquadratic in the exponent. Its zeros  and  identify the constant\nand the inverse-power harmonic profiles.\n\nAt every positive radius, the dimension- radial Laplacian sends the power profile  to\n. For , the choice  gives the harmonic\ninverse-power profile. In dimension two this choice gives only the constant\nprofile; the nonconstant logarithmic profile is covered by the preceding theorem.\n\nOn , the first two derivatives of  are\n and . Adding the radial correction\n and collecting coefficients yields\n.\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Foundations\u0009Coordinates and Boundary Calculus\u0009Euclidean, polar, and spherical coordinates","header":"3.3.1.2. Radial and planar differential coordinates","id":"/Foundations/#PDEAtlas--Foundations--Coordinates-and-Boundary-Calculus--Euclidean___-polar___-and-spherical-coordinates--Radial-and-planar-differential-coordinates"},"/Hyperbolic-Equations/#PDEAtlas--Hyperbolic-Equations--Coverage-and-Open-Problems--Characteristics___-Waves___-and-Nonlinear-Models--Propagation-along-characteristics":{"contents":"We begin with transport and the one-dimensional wave equation. D'Alembert's\nformula satisfies the wave equation and both initial identities under\nexplicit smoothness assumptions. Energy identities then give the domain of\ndependence and finite propagation. In higher dimensions the Kirchhoff,\nPoisson, and general odd- and even-dimensional formulas solve the Cauchy\nproblem with both joint initial traces. Duhamel's principle adds a forcing\nterm with zero Cauchy data in every positive spatial dimension, including\nthe one-dimensional retarded d'Alembert formula.\n\nFor semilinear waves, the local energy balance yields two complementary\nconclusions. Shrinking-ball transport gives finite propagation when the\npotential is nonnegative and vanishes at zero. On all of space, a normalized\n potential and a  wave with separately compact displacement\nslices give conservation of total energy in every positive dimension.\nThe proof derives common support on finite slabs, including control of the\nactual initial right velocity. For a normalized  potential,\nconservation is proved in one and two dimensions with  and \nwaves respectively, and in every positive dimension with a  wave\nand a quadratic lower bound on the potential near zero. A nonnegative\npotential makes the conserved energy control the kinetic, spatial-gradient,\nand potential terms separately.\n\nA separate construction starts from data in \non a nonempty bounded open domain in positive dimension. With an autonomous\nbounded measurable symmetric uniformly elliptic principal matrix and a\nlocally Lipschitz map from this energy space to ,\nthe semigroup construction gives a\nmaximal continuous energy-space trajectory and its weak wave\nequation on every compact initial interval before the lifetime. Both initial\nvalues are attained. Uniqueness holds among weak solutions represented by\ncontinuous energy-space curves. Finite-lifetime norm blowup additionally\nrequires a Lipschitz bound on every bounded energy ball. Nonlinear energy\nconservation and the estimates for specific power nonlinearities require\nfurther hypotheses and arguments.\n\nOn , a globally Lipschitz scalar  with  gives\na global forward solution of  for arbitrary\n data. Both strong initial traces and the integrated\nweak PDE are proved, with uniqueness among energy-continuous PDE solutions;\nsee .\nThis is an energy-space theorem. For smooth compact data in three dimensions,\nthe construction below gives classical solutions and a lifespan alternative.\n\nFor  Cauchy data on  and any smooth scalar\nreaction fixing zero, a separate construction gives a maximal smooth wave;\nsee .\nIf its lifespan is finite, displacement is unbounded on every terminal\ntime strip.\n\nFor nonlinear first-order equations, let the boundary parametrization \nand values  be  on an open neighborhood of , and let  be\n near the initial phase . Assume  satisfies\nthe PDE and tangential compatibility conditions and that the initial\ntangent/characteristic map is invertible. If  lies in the prescribed\nopen ambient domain, we construct a  solution on a smaller open spatial\nneighborhood within that domain, with , trace \nnear , and base gradient .\n\nThe implicit function theorem constructs a compatible  momentum field.\nThe  characteristic flow and its first variation propagate the\ncontact identity. Inverse characteristic coordinates then\nidentify  with a  momentum field, giving . A global\n representative of the equation near the initial phase supplies the\nconstruction; restricting the solution back to the agreement neighborhood\nrecovers the original . Two  solutions with the same boundary\nvalues and the same base momentum agree near the basepoint.\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Hyperbolic Equations\u0009Coverage and Open Problems\u0009Characteristics, Waves, and Nonlinear Models","header":"7.6.1.1. Propagation along characteristics","id":"/Hyperbolic-Equations/#PDEAtlas--Hyperbolic-Equations--Coverage-and-Open-Problems--Characteristics___-Waves___-and-Nonlinear-Models--Propagation-along-characteristics"},"/Incompressible-Fluid-Mechanics/#PDEAtlas--Incompressible-Fluid-Mechanics":{"contents":"In Eulerian fluid mechanics the velocity field has two roles at once: it is the\nunknown motion, and it is the field that transports that motion. This makes the\ntransport term nonlinear. Written out, the equation is Newton's\nlaw for a continuum,\n\n\n\nwith the material acceleration balanced by pressure, viscous diffusion, and any\nexternal forcing, and incompressibility saying that fluid volumes are preserved.\nSet  and it is Euler; drop the nonlinear transport term and it is Stokes.\nThe equation brings together several earlier subjects: advection is\nhyperbolic, viscosity is parabolic, and pressure is not evolved at all but\nrecovered from an elliptic constraint.\n\nTwo explicit flows separate these roles. In\nsolid rotation, the velocity turns along\ncircular streamlines, and a quadratic pressure balances the centripetal\nacceleration. In the forced sine shear,\nadvection vanishes and forcing balances viscous diffusion. Each example tests\nindividual terms of the general equation, and its CAS certificate proves the\nresidual identities exactly.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas","header":"8. Incompressible Fluid Mechanics","id":"/Incompressible-Fluid-Mechanics/#PDEAtlas--Incompressible-Fluid-Mechanics"},"/Verified-Symbolic-and-Continuous-Computation/#PDEAtlas--Verified-Symbolic-and-Continuous-Computation--Validated-Solvers-and-Certificate-Workflows":{"contents":"\n\n\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Verified Symbolic and Continuous Computation","header":"10.9. Validated Solvers and Certificate Workflows","id":"/Verified-Symbolic-and-Continuous-Computation/#PDEAtlas--Verified-Symbolic-and-Continuous-Computation--Validated-Solvers-and-Certificate-Workflows"}});